1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,772
Hi, I'm Mohammed Alibhai.

2
00:00:01,772 --> 00:00:05,043
For Track one of the Global Innovation Build Challenge,

3
00:00:05,043 --> 00:00:07,422
I trained a language model from scratch.

4
00:00:07,422 --> 00:00:11,307
The cap is fifty million parameters, and mine has just under that.

5
00:00:11,307 --> 00:00:16,166
It learned from random weights on twenty billion tokens of openly licensed English

6
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:20,372
text, with my own tokenizer, on one H one hundred in about eight hours.

7
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:22,117
So, the model.

8
00:00:22,117 --> 00:00:26,175
It's a fourteen-layer transformer, five hundred and twelve wide, with

9
00:00:26,175 --> 00:00:30,467
grouped-query attention, rotary embeddings, Q-K norm, and a squared ReLU.

10
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:34,235
U-net style skips connect early layers to late ones, and a value

11
00:00:34,235 --> 00:00:37,590
residual lets every layer reuse the first layer's values.

12
00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:41,010
The thirty-two-thousand-entry embedding is shared with the

13
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:43,604
output head and takes a third of the budget.

14
00:00:43,604 --> 00:00:47,867
Counted once, as one tensor, the total is forty-nine point eight million.

15
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:50,669
Counted twice, it's sixty-six point six million.

16
00:00:50,669 --> 00:00:51,487
I report both.

17
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,312
Next, training.

18
00:00:53,312 --> 00:00:55,869
I used the modded nano-GPT speedrun recipe.

19
00:00:55,869 --> 00:00:58,877
Muon (MEW-on) updates the block matrices, and Adam

20
00:00:58,877 --> 00:01:01,043
W handles the embedding and scalars.

21
00:01:01,043 --> 00:01:05,116
The learning rate warms up, holds, then decays to zero over the last

22
00:01:05,116 --> 00:01:09,308
thirty-five percent, when the data switches to higher-quality sources.

23
00:01:09,309 --> 00:01:14,244
Averaging the last few snapshots made no difference, so I report the final weights.

24
00:01:14,244 --> 00:01:18,229
Near the end, both runs stopped when I hit my cloud spending limit.

25
00:01:18,229 --> 00:01:21,112
They resumed exactly from their last checkpoint,

26
00:01:21,112 --> 00:01:24,175
optimizer, random state and data position included.

27
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:29,062
On to the data: FineWeb-Edu, D-C-L-M, FineMath, FinePDFs and Wikipedia,

28
00:01:29,062 --> 00:01:32,187
all openly licensed, none of it intentionally synthetic.

29
00:01:32,187 --> 00:01:36,450
WikiText one-oh-three comes from Wikipedia, which is copied all over the web.

30
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:39,136
Before filtering, about five percent of its test

31
00:01:39,136 --> 00:01:41,821
thirteen-grams turned up in our training sample.

32
00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:46,447
So we drop every document sharing a thirteen-gram with WikiText validation or test,

33
00:01:46,447 --> 00:01:50,182
just under five thousand, plus sixteen Wikipedia articles by title.

34
00:01:50,182 --> 00:01:53,259
The multiple-choice benchmarks aren't filtered, but the

35
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:55,442
measured overlap is a handful of items.

36
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:00,016
Before spending eight GPU hours on a main run, we ran twelve

37
00:02:00,016 --> 00:02:03,349
one-billion-token experiments, each changing one thing.

38
00:02:03,349 --> 00:02:05,391
Muon beat Adam W by a wide margin.

39
00:02:05,391 --> 00:02:09,750
The value residual gave the biggest perplexity gain, fifty-seven down to

40
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:13,141
fifty-four, for thirteen extra parameters, so I kept it.

41
00:02:13,141 --> 00:02:16,235
The quality anneal raised ARC-Easy but lowered PIQA

42
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:19,208
(PIE-kah), so my two main runs try two strengths.

43
00:02:19,208 --> 00:02:23,954
With one seed each, gaps under two points are noise, so I leaned on perplexity.

44
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:28,893
These are the submitted model's final numbers, after all twenty billion tokens.

45
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:30,730
HellaSwag, about thirty-two percent.

46
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:32,109
ARC-Easy, about fifty-five.

47
00:02:32,109 --> 00:02:33,232
PIQA, about sixty-two.

48
00:02:33,232 --> 00:02:35,529
WinoGrande, about fifty-two, close to chance.

49
00:02:35,529 --> 00:02:37,519
WikiText perplexity, about thirty-nine.

50
00:02:37,519 --> 00:02:40,764
Against the one-billion-token run, ARC-Easy is up eight points,

51
00:02:40,764 --> 00:02:43,339
and perplexity fell from sixty-one to thirty-nine.

52
00:02:43,339 --> 00:02:46,124
The PIQA drop from the strong anneal didn't show up at

53
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:48,188
twenty billion, so I submitted that run.

54
00:02:48,188 --> 00:02:52,068
All zero-shot, on the full evaluation sets, with the L-M evaluation harness.

55
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:55,891
And now the model itself, running locally on my laptop.

56
00:02:55,891 --> 00:02:58,220
I give it three prompts and let it continue.

57
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,867
It's grammatical and keeps the tone of the prompt.

58
00:03:00,867 --> 00:03:04,071
It also gets facts wrong and repeats itself, and in my other

59
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:06,848
samples it couldn't do a simple apples word problem.

60
00:03:06,848 --> 00:03:09,547
That's what I'd expect at fifty million parameters.

61
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:17,496
Finally, everything needed to reproduce this is in the repository.

62
00:03:17,496 --> 00:03:20,708
U-V installs the pinned environment, and the Modal

63
00:03:20,708 --> 00:03:23,471
scripts build the data, train and evaluate.

64
00:03:23,471 --> 00:03:27,731
On AI use: I built this with Claude Code agents under my direction.

65
00:03:27,731 --> 00:03:32,054
The agents wrote the code, ran the experiments and drafted the docs.

66
00:03:32,054 --> 00:03:36,348
I set the goals, made the decisions, reviewed the work and paid for

67
00:03:36,348 --> 00:03:39,873
compute, and I can explain the code and design choices.

68
00:03:39,873 --> 00:03:41,144
Thanks for watching.

